I Didn't Steal the Admin Password.
I Asked the Database to Tell Me Where It Was Hiding It.

Today I completed PortSwigger's SQL Injection: Listing Database Contents on Non-Oracle Databases lab.
What made this lab different wasn't the final login as administrator.
It was the process.
At the start, all I had was a product category filter.
No user table.
No password table.
No credentials.
Just a single input field.
Then the investigation began.

Questions I Had to Answer
- How many columns does the query return?
- Which columns can display text?
- What tables exist in the database?
- Which table stores user credentials?
- Which columns contain usernames and passwords?

Each answer unlocked the next question.
One payload became several.
One clue led to another.
Eventually, I was able to enumerate the database structure, identify the credentials table, extract user records, and obtain the administrator password.

The most valuable thing I gained wasn't the administrator password.
It was learning how to think methodically when information isn't handed to me.
Every table discovered.
Every column identified.
Every assumption tested.
Every payload written for a reason.

